WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.767
Hi, I'm Runnel!

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:07.698
Here is how Microsoft's NVX gives each agent workload its own tiny virtual machine.

00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:09.681
The idea.

00:00:09.681 --> 00:00:11.548
Do not trim a whole PC.

00:00:11.548 --> 00:00:14.972
Build only the machine you allow, the same on every hypervisor.

00:00:17.608 --> 00:00:21.348
A general monitor emulates a PC, with firmware and a PCI bus,

00:00:21.348 --> 00:00:22.906
and you switch parts off.

00:00:23.326 --> 00:00:29.064
NVX boots Linux directly, puts six devices at fixed addresses, and rejects everything else.

00:00:30.758 --> 00:00:35.517
The host decides policy, and hands it to OpenVMM, which checks everything twice.

00:00:35.937 --> 00:00:39.775
One machine runs on three hypervisors, with no paravirtual clock.

00:00:40.195 --> 00:00:44.314
Inside, guest agents find the fixed devices and build the workload's root.

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:48.317
The workload runs as a non root user with no capabilities, and

00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:50.108
the agent cleans up after it.

00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:53.139
Fast starts come from snapshots.

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:57.111
The guest asks for a snapshot, and its machine stops for good.

00:00:57.531 --> 00:01:01.639
Each restore maps the saved memory privately, so clones keep their own writes.

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:06.100
The host sends sixty four fresh random bytes, so clones never share keys.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.215
The guest fixes its clock before any workload runs.

00:01:10.758 --> 00:01:13.639
The network card offers only two features.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.697
No checksum or segmentation offload.

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:19.282
Frames reach a network stack inside the monitor, checked twice.

00:01:19.702 --> 00:01:22.604
Allowed traffic leaves through an ordinary host socket.

00:01:22.604 --> 00:01:23.692
No tap device.

00:01:25.008 --> 00:01:28.977
On the project's own test machines, a cold start takes about

00:01:28.977 --> 00:01:30.420
two hundred eighty milliseconds.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.864
A restore, about sixty.

00:01:33.058 --> 00:01:33.740
The takeaway.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.150
The isolation comes from what this machine refuses to have.

00:01:39.608 --> 00:01:40.288
To recap.

00:01:40.288 --> 00:01:44.029
Build the machine, restore with fresh entropy, keep the network small.

00:01:44.449 --> 00:01:46.475
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:46.475 --> 00:01:47.054
I'm Runnel.

00:01:47.054 --> 00:01:47.344
Bye!
